<article class="env-lemma" id="01C3">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01C3">Lemma <span data-tag="01C3">17.13.2</span>.</a> Let $i : (Z, \mathcal{O}_ Z) \to (X, \mathcal{O}_ X)$ be a closed immersion of ringed spaces. Let $\mathcal{F}$ be a quasi-coherent $\mathcal{O}_ Z$-module. Then $i_*\mathcal{F}$ is locally on $X$ the cokernel of a map of quasi-coherent $\mathcal{O}_ X$-modules. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This is true because $i_*\mathcal{O}_ Z$ is quasi-coherent by definition. And locally on $Z$ the sheaf $\mathcal{F}$ is a cokernel of a map between direct sums of copies of $\mathcal{O}_ Z$. Moreover, any direct sum of copies of the <em>the same</em> quasi-coherent sheaf is quasi-coherent. And finally, $i_*$ commutes with arbitrary colimits, see Lemma <a href="/tag/01AZ" data-tag="01AZ">17.6.3</a>. Some details omitted. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>